    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** and **Trea Turner** headlining our lineup, both benefiting significantly from favorable matchups and high-upside speed dynamics. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.27) is our strongest play, facing a soft-tossing Patrick Corbin; despite the platoon disadvantage, his elite sprint speed against a high-walk rate lefty provides a prime stolen base environment. **Trea Turner** (67.77) similarly grades out as an elite start; while he faces a tough Freddy Peralta, the lack of a quick move to the plate from the mound allows **Turner** to exploit the basepaths, bolstering his value beyond raw contact metrics. **Yordan Alvarez** (66.32) and **Kyle Schwarber** (62.23) round out the top tier, with **Alvarez** maintaining a massive 0.450 xwOBA that necessitates a start regardless of his platoon disadvantage against Joey Cantillo.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Carson Benge** (38.60), who narrowly missed the cut behind **Casey Schmitt** (44.51). While **Benge** offers upside, his matchup against a high-tier arm like Cristopher S\u00e1nchez forced the optimizer to favor **Schmitt**, whose dynamic platoon advantage against a right-handed pitcher provides a safer floor for our efficiency targets. We are closely monitoring the status of **Christian Yelich** (42.50) and **Caleb Durbin** (51.03); both are currently projected in our starting lineup, but their final scores are sensitive to the official confirmation of their batting order. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Yelich**, whose score is suppressed by a difficult matchup against Chris Sale, and we have opted to bench **Gary Sanchez** (38.32) entirely due to a poor BvP track record against the same southpaw. As we navigate the scarcity of the SB category, the aggression shown by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** and **Trea Turner** remains our primary engine for points today.",
